message.content:
{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS climate scenario disclosures, has ABN Amro confirmed that the assumptions underpinning its IFRS financial statements are consistent with the climate pathway used in its scenario assessment?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro states that the assumptions used in preparing its IFRS financial statements are consistent with the same baseline scenario applied in its assessment."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within ABN AMRO’s risk taxonomy, is sustainability risk explicitly designated as a material risk category?",
      "answer": "Yes. The disclosure states that sustainability risk is treated as a material risk in ABN AMRO’s risk taxonomy."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within its disclosure on current and anticipated effects, does ABN AMRO explicitly report the outcome of a resilience assessment (via a bank-wide ICAAP stress test) demonstrating that projected climate and environmental risk impacts over the assessed five-year horizon remain within its loss-absorbing capacity and that its business model is resilient to the adverse scenario?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO states that a bank-wide ICAAP stress test assessed the financial impact of environmental and related risks over a five-year horizon, found the effects to be well within its loss-absorbing capacity, and concluded that its business model is sufficiently resilient to the adverse scenario."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within its description of environmental impacts, does ABN AMRO specify that the principal adverse effects occur through client-related financing and investment activities rather than from its own direct operations?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO indicates that the majority of its environmental impacts are associated with its engagements with clients—through lending, investment products, and other services—while its own operational footprint is described as minimal."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN AMRO, does the stated scope of its social impact assessment explicitly cover impacts stemming from its own operations as well as those associated with its clients’ activities?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO indicates it addresses social impacts both within its operations and through its clients’ activities."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Does ABN Amro state that its updated Standard with Client Requirements uses terminology harmonised with the CSRD and the ESRS?",
      "answer": "Yes. The chunk specifies that, while the Generic Principles remain unchanged, ABN Amro enhanced the underlying Standard with Client Requirements so that it aligns with the terminology used in the CSRD and the related ESRS."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro, does the disclosure confirm that results from the Client Assessment on Sustainability (CASY) and the Climate and Environmental Risk Classification Tool (RCT) are embedded in corporate credit risk evaluation, including second-line review and ultimate credit decision-making?",
      "answer": "Yes. It states that CASY and RCT outputs are taken into account in the credit risk assessment, are subject to a second-line review, and inform credit risk decisions for corporate clients."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For client-level risk mitigation, does ABN Amro disclose that its high-intensity engagement track is subject to quarterly review by the Engagement Committee, evidencing periodic internal oversight?",
      "answer": "Yes. The description states that high-intensity engagements follow a formal, time-bound process, are closely monitored by relationship managers and the Credit Risk Sustainability unit, and receive quarterly oversight from the Engagement Committee."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Has ABN Amro clearly specified that the Climate & Environmental Risks (CER) materiality assessment covers the entirety of its balance sheet?",
      "answer": "Yes. The disclosure states that the assessment’s coverage includes the bank’s full balance-sheet positions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within its 2024 risk appetite framework, has ABN Amro established explicit caps or trigger points to limit exposure to mortgage collateral situated in areas facing elevated physical climate risk?",
      "answer": "Yes. In 2024, ABN Amro incorporated climate-related credit risk indicators into its Risk Appetite Statement and set limits and checkpoints for vulnerable mortgage collateral in areas with increased physical risk."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro’s ESRS materiality determination of climate and environmental drivers on market risk in the trading book, does the disclosure set out the assessment method used to gauge potential profit-and-loss effects and the specific rationale supporting its conclusion that this risk is not material?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro assessed potential profit-and-loss effects from revaluation of mark-to-market trading positions by running stress scenarios that shocked market risk factors, including credit spreads. It concluded the impact is not material because the trading book bond holdings are concentrated in government securities and high-grade financial issuers that exhibit low sensitivity to physical and transition climate risks; therefore, ABN Amro assesses climate and environmental market risk in the trading book as immaterial."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within ABN Amro’s climate and environmental risk materiality review for liquidity risk, what specific short- and medium-term horizons are set for the quantitative assessment?",
      "answer": "Short term is set as within 1 year, and medium term is set as within 5 years."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "In its 2024 climate and environmental business-risk assessment, does ABN Amro state that transition risk is not material in the near term but could become material over the medium to long run due to prospective changes to its commercial or risk policies to meet climate-related requirements?",
      "answer": "Yes. It indicates transition risk is not material in the short term, while in the medium to long term it could become material as adjustments to commercial or risk policies are made to curb other climate and environmental risks or to lower client-portfolio emissions in line with climate-related regulations."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Does ABN Amro’s adverse scenario explicitly include a Dutch policy tightening on buildings with poor energy performance that would further depress the market value of such real estate?",
      "answer": "Yes. The scenario states that the Dutch government would introduce more stringent requirements for less energy‑efficient real estate, which would push their prices down further."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within its CER discussion of strategy and business model, does ABN Amro indicate whether lending decisions in its residential mortgage portfolio are conditioned on physical climate-risk metrics, and if not, what rationale does it provide?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro reports that physical risks are concentrated in its residential mortgages and that it does not condition or differentiate lending on the basis of physical risk indicators, citing its social role in facilitating home ownership."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro, does the non-financial risk stress-testing methodology used for ESG materiality explicitly assess climate and environmental risk events across three defined time horizons (1, 5 and 30 years)?",
      "answer": "Yes. It evaluates potentially high-impact non-financial risk events related to climate and environmental risks over 1-year, 5-year and 30-year horizons."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "In its stated assessment outcome, does ABN Amro confirm that a flooding-related data centre disruption is material in the short-term (one-year) horizon and specify the selected risk treatment?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro indicates that a data centre outage due to flood risk could materially affect its financial results and reputation within a one-year horizon, and it has adopted a monitoring approach while the flood exposure of its data centres is being investigated."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Does ABN Amro’s disclosure confirm that corporate clients are evaluated against the bank’s baseline sustainability criteria both at onboarding and at recurring intervals thereafter?",
      "answer": "Yes. It states that when corporate clients are onboarded and then periodically, ABN Amro conducts a dialogue to determine whether they meet the bank’s minimum sustainability-related standards and overarching corporate principles."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS disclosures on risk management processes, what client coverage threshold does ABN Amro state for applying its CASY sustainability assessment?",
      "answer": "CASY is applied to corporate clients whose credit exposure exceeds EUR 1 million."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro’s non-programme lending clients, is there a process within credit origination and subsequent review cycles that integrates climate- and environment-related risk exposures into the creditworthiness assessment?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro uses its CER Risk Classification Tool (RCT), which, via a questionnaire, classifies each non-programme lending client into a low-to-high CER risk profile and incorporates physical climate, transition and environmental risk considerations into creditworthiness assessments at origination and during periodic reviews. The data gathered are retained for reuse within the credit risk process."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS-aligned scoping of climate-risk analysis, does ABN AMRO identify the specific industries where its transmission-channel assessments have been strengthened and clarify that the referenced NACE classifications are indicative mappings to its own sector definitions?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO reports that it has reinforced its transmission-channel evaluations for agriculture/forestry/fishing, oil and gas extraction, metals and chemicals manufacturing, power generation, construction, transport (shipping, aviation, and road), and real estate. It also explains that the NACE codes cited are used as indicative references and do not replace the bank’s internal sector taxonomy."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN AMRO, which governance body is charged with reviewing sectoral financing policies that establish exposure targets and limits linked to climate and environmental risk?",
      "answer": "ABN AMRO’s Group Risk Committee (GRC) performs this review as part of its sustainability risk oversight."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Which ABN AMRO governance body is tasked with supervising the soundness and quality of the bank’s sustainability statements and its public sustainability disclosures?",
      "answer": "ABN AMRO’s Audit Committee is responsible for this oversight, monitoring the quality of the bank’s sustainability statements and external disclosures as part of its supervision of financial and sustainability reporting."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Does ABN Amro’s disclosure confirm that its sustainability risk policy framework encompasses climate change mitigation and the management of both physical and transition climate exposures?",
      "answer": "Yes. The section states that the sustainability risk policy framework covers mitigation of climate change and the management of both transition and physical climate risks, helping the bank address broader climate impacts."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Within ABN Amro’s governance of operational climate and energy measures, has responsibility for policy execution been allocated to named executive roles, and which roles are assigned to the Environmental and Energy Policy and the Travel and Mobility policy?",
      "answer": "Yes. The Environmental and Energy Policy is under the execution remit of the Chief Operations Officer, while the Travel and Mobility policy is executed under the Chief Human Resources Officer."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN AMRO’s exposure to coal-fired power generation clients, does the stated sustainability risk standard include a time-bound commitment to exit thermal coal, and if so, what is the deadline year?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO reports it has updated its sustainability risk standard for coal-fired power generation clients to include client requirements that commit to winding down thermal coal by 2030."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "In its sector target overview, does ABN AMRO quantify what share of its outstanding exposure to NZBA-identified high-emission sectors is already within the scope of its published climate targets?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO states that 95% of its total exposure to NZBA high-emission sectors is covered by its climate targets, calculated using gross carrying-amount exposures; the remaining 5% will be incorporated in later strategy updates or is excluded due to methodological considerations."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Has ABN Amro specified the methodological scope and inclusion criteria for counting financing toward its lending commitment on renewable energy and decarbonisation technologies?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro measures the commitment on lending exposures including both drawn and undrawn amounts, and applies a two-part scope: (1) a renewable-energy component that covers activities, assets, infrastructure, projects and companies involved in developing, constructing, installing, manufacturing, maintaining, operating or advising on renewable energy and related storage; and (2) a decarbonisation-technologies component that covers activities relating to renewable generation, hydrogen manufacturing, energy storage, clean transport and related infrastructure within ABN Amro’s Corporate Bank financing, provided they are not already included in the renewable-energy component."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS reporting on workforce scope, does ABN AMRO state that its HR Risk Policy extends beyond its own employees to include, where pertinent, non-employee personnel engaged through third-party suppliers?",
      "answer": "Yes. The HR Risk Policy applies to employees and, where relevant, to external and other non-employees working with ABN AMRO via third-party suppliers."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Has ABN AMRO put in place a formal human-rights due diligence process designed to oversee and reduce adverse human-rights impacts arising from its activities?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO states it has implemented a human-rights due diligence process specifically to manage and lessen human-rights impacts."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN AMRO, does the remuneration framework explicitly tie sustainability performance to incentive compensation across employee categories, and how is this operationalized for CLA+, CLA Identified Staff, CLA staff, and the Executive Board?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO awards individual variable pay based on pre-set annual KPIs. For CLA+ and CLA Identified Staff, sustainability criteria are built directly into personal KPIs via Sustainability Assets and the climate strategy. For CLA staff, sustainability factors influence the size of the discretionary variable remuneration pool. For the Executive Board, sustainability is reflected through KPIs linked to the Dow Jones Sustainability Index and the climate strategy."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Has ABN Amro disclosed that it maintains ongoing dialogue with civil society groups to address human rights risks associated with its investment activities?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro states that it holds regular discussions with civil society groups to address human rights risks connected to its investment activities."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Identify the specific report in which ABN AMRO indicates it provides further detail on its arrangements to facilitate remedy access for value chain workers within its procurement activities.",
      "answer": "ABN AMRO states that its Integrated Annual Report 2024 provides more information on how it facilitates access to remedy for value chain workers and on its approach to actions for those workers."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro, does the disclosure specify the interim method used to monitor the effectiveness of its policies and actions—namely, evaluating its influence over corporate clients and businesses in its investment sphere to derive insights on engagement-related impacts and risks—given that formal targets have not yet been set?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro states that, although formal targets are not yet established, it currently evaluates how effectively it can exercise leverage with corporate clients and businesses within its investment sphere, and uses these assessments to gain insight into the impacts and risks of its engagement efforts."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS reporting on grievance access for value‑chain workers, which specific mechanism does ABN AMRO indicate is available to workers in its supply chain?",
      "answer": "ABN AMRO identifies the International Framework Agreement (IFA) as the grievance channel available to workers in its supply chain."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS-consistent reporting on consumers and end-users, does ABN Amro evidence that it maintains a consumer-facing grievance mechanism that enables remediation when adverse human rights impacts to which the bank has contributed or with which it is directly connected are identified?",
      "answer": "Yes. The text states that ABN AMRO provides or enables access to remedy through its consumer complaints mechanism whenever negative human rights impacts that it contributes to or is directly linked to are identified, and refers to the section of its Integrated Annual Report 2024 where this mechanism is outlined."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS reporting on privacy safeguards for consumers and end-users, does ABN Amro’s Personal Data Policy impose a formal obligation to inform affected consumers when a personal data breach occurs?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro’s Personal Data Policy sets minimum standards for all staff and expressly requires that consumers be notified in the event of a personal data breach."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Under ABN AMRO’s Product Approval and Review policy, is there a formal requirement to update products and services to take account of evolving sustainability-related developments?",
      "answer": "Yes. The policy requires offerings to be updated in line with changes in the macroeconomic environment, societal factors, and sustainability matters, while remaining compliant with applicable regulations and aligned with the bank’s ESG objectives."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN AMRO Bank N.V., is the complaints oversight function explicitly established as an independent remit within the Legal department, tasked with designing, updating, supervising the Complaints Management Policy and holding overall ownership of the complaints-handling process?",
      "answer": "Yes. The Complaints Management department located in Legal has an independent mandate to develop, review and oversee the Complaints Management Policy and it retains overall ownership of the bank’s complaints-handling process."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "At ABN Amro, does the product oversight process formally use customer complaint insights within its product approval/review cycle to adjust product features or the intended customer segment?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro states that product-related complaints are fed into its review feedback loop and used to refine product features and the definition of the intended customer segment."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Has ABN Amro documented targeted engagement with people with physical disabilities during the reporting period to evaluate and enhance the accessibility of its banking products and services?",
      "answer": "Yes. The disclosure states that in 2024 ABN Amro proactively consulted clients with physical disabilities to assess and improve the accessibility of its products and services, alongside using methods such as market studies, customer panels, discussion groups, questionnaires, and individual interviews."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ESRS governance-control disclosures, does ABN AMRO state that it performs periodic internal control activities to assess whether its Client Integrity policies are properly implemented and effective?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN AMRO reports that it has processes and control frameworks in place, including policy adherence indicators and key controls, which are used on a periodic basis to monitor and test the implementation and effectiveness of its Client Integrity policies."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro’s disclosure of financed emissions tied to its corporate lending, has the bank explicitly adopted a methodological presumption that borrowers producing electricity from solar, wind, or hydro have zero Scope 1 and Scope 2 emission intensity, and does it articulate the basis for that presumption?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro states that clients engaged in renewable power generation from solar, wind, or hydropower are assigned zero intensity for Scopes 1 and 2, justified by the nature of these activities, which the bank explains entail no associated direct or purchased-energy emissions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For its road-transport portfolio, does ABN Amro identify the specific data sources used to derive vehicle activity and the country-dependent emission factors for trucks and vans?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro reports that it uses TRACCS for vehicle-activity data across all countries and relies on TRACCS for emission factors in the UK and Germany, while CE Delft provides the emission factors for the Netherlands."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro’s power generation portfolio, does the bank disclose both the origin of the data used to compute emissions intensity and the measure it applies to align all inputs to a consistent reporting year?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro states that it uses data provided directly by its clients to calculate the portfolio’s emissions intensity and applies a one-year reporting lag to ensure all inputs are sourced from the same year."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN AMRO, what reporting lag is disclosed for the operational emissions-intensity metrics in the oil and gas upstream and midstream portfolio?",
      "answer": "ABN AMRO indicates these intensity figures are reported with a one-year delay."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro, is the percentage gap to its net-zero-by-2050 pathway disclosed for the financed emissions intensities of trucks and vans used in road transport?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro reports a 31% gap for trucks and a 55% gap for vans relative to its NZE 2050 pathway."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro’s agriculture disclosures, does the bank state that, due to the Pillar 3 counterparty boundary, household borrowers in this portfolio are omitted from the template?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro explains that the Pillar 3 counterparty scope affects the agriculture figures because part of the portfolio consists of households, which are therefore not included in the template."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For ABN Amro’s physical risk methodology, does a specific quantitative criterion apply to Dutch flooding cases to flag an exposure as acute flood–sensitive, and if so, what is that criterion?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro applies a Netherlands-specific rule: an exposure is deemed acute flood–sensitive if at least 10% of buildings in the relevant NUTS region face flood depths exceeding 0.5 metres."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "In its GAR KPI summary, does ABN Amro state that it cannot compute the GAR flow “coverage over total assets” metric because the flow template lacks the total-asset denominator needed for that calculation?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro explains that determining the coverage percentage for flow is not practicable since the flow template contains only GAR assets and omits the total assets required to derive a coverage ratio."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For its Green Asset Ratio disclosures, does ABN Amro state that exposures to financial counterparties outside the NFRD scope are recorded under the Pillar 3 line item \"other assets\" in order to keep the presentation consistent with the EU Taxonomy’s separate category for such exposures?",
      "answer": "Yes. ABN Amro explains that, because the Pillar 3 template has no dedicated line for exposures to non‑NFRD financial corporations, these exposures are reported under \"other assets\" so the disclosures can be reconciled with the EU Taxonomy, where they appear as a distinct line item."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "For the GAR disclosures, did ABN AMRO provide EU Taxonomy ‘flow’ KPI figures for the reporting dates 31 December 2023 and 30 June 2024, or did it explicitly defer these metrics to a later reporting period?",
      "answer": "ABN AMRO did not provide the EU Taxonomy flow KPI figures for both 31 December 2023 and 30 June 2024. The notes state that these figures are left blank and will start being reported from Q4 2024 to align with the approach used for the EU Taxonomy disclosures in the Integrated Annual Report 2024."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "In its disclosure of non-Taxonomy climate-mitigation financing, does ABN Amro indicate that its loan exposures (including those to financial and non-financial corporates, commercial real estate, households, and residential mortgages) mitigate physical climate risk?",
      "answer": "No. The template specifies 'No' for physical climate risk mitigation across all loan categories—financial corporations, non-financial corporations, loans collateralised by commercial immovable property, households, and loans collateralised by residential immovable property—while physical risk mitigation is indicated as 'Yes' only for bonds."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "In its ESRS-focused risk disclosures, has BBVA stated that its Risk Appetite Framework sets Board-approved quantitative caps on exposure-at-default to activities internally assessed as High Transition Risk?",
      "answer": "Yes. BBVA discloses that its Risk Appetite Framework, applicable across the Group, classifies activities by transition sensitivity using internal quantitative metrics and measures the exposure-at-default to those deemed High Transition Risk. The BBVA Board of Directors approves thresholds at Group and relevant geography level that cap the maximum appetite for such exposures, with tolerance levels informed by the bank’s risk assessment and scenario analysis. In 2024, BBVA also added an indicator linked to progress against sectoral decarbonization targets and monitors clients with emission intensities materially above market trajectories through a separate misalignment metric."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "chunk": "",
  "chunk_id": "",
  "bank_name": "",
  "questions": [
    {
